 The ambiguous part: Hermes MCP login verification                                                                      
                                                                                                                        
 What I proved along the way:                                                                                           
 - hermes mcp add with OAuth is interactive/TUI-only, so the entry was written directly to config.yaml (same shape the  
 CLI saves): url, auth: oauth, ssl_verify. The ssl_verify line is essential — verified empirically that httpx (the MCP  
 client stack) fails on the private CA with default settings but passes with the explicit path; system Python doesn't   
 hit this because it reads capath                                                                                       
 - Hermes' DCR against our Keycloak works (201s), auth URL is correct (PKCE S256, mcp:tools + offline_access, loopback  
 callback, prompt=consent)                                                                                              
 - A headless driver (/tmp/kc-browser-flow.py) reliably walks login form → consent screen through nginx and captures    
 the final redirect                                                                                                     
                                                                                                                        
 The blocker is a Hermes behavior, not Keycloak: every hermes mcp login attempt dies with "OAuth callback port 27890 is 
 already in use". The process runs two OAuth flows internally (same client_id, two states) and the second bind fails    
 while the first's single-shot listener is still bound — so whatever completes the browser step races a dead flow.      
 Evidence: my own agent session was one of those racers (config auto-reload + background MCP discovery re-triggered     
 OAuth for camera-new; logs show its attempts). An isolated probe proved isolation works: with HERMES_HOME=/tmp/hh2 and 
 auto-reload disabled, exactly one auth URL appeared and port 27890 was held solely by the login's own process.         
 What remains to close out                                                                                              

                                                                                                                        
 1. Run env -u DISPLAY HERMES_HOME=/tmp/hh2 hermes mcp login camera-new (PTY, no browser auto-open), drive              
 /tmp/kc-browser-flow.py against its URL, paste the captured redirect into Hermes' stdin fallback                       
 2. Copy /tmp/hh2/mcp-tokens/camera-new.* → ~/.hermes/mcp-tokens/, enable camera-new, run hermes mcp test camera-new    
 (the doc's verification step) — this completes DCR + browser auth + reconnect-with-saved-state and tool discovery      
 3. Cleanup: orphan DCR clients accumulated from failed login attempts (several UUIDs in the mcp realm — resolve each   
 by name before deletion), re-enable auto-reload or keep it off deliberately, remove /tmp/hh2                           
 4. Per section 14's note: take a post-login backup so the real client registration is captured                         
